<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ghazan Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1304">1304</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Mahmud Ghazan, Ghazan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ghazan Khan, also known as Mahmud Ghazan, was ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> who died in 1304. He converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> on taking power, making it the religion of the Mongol state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, and with his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-al-din-hamadani">Rashid al-Din</a> carried out wide reforms of taxation, coinage and administration. His reign is regarded as a high point of the Ilkhanate.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ghazan-khan.jpg" alt="Ghazan Khan" width="320" height="387" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown Mongol author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ghazan_with_wife_at_his_court.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Recovery began around the turn of the fourteenth century under <b>Ghazan Khan</b> (r. 1295–1304), whose fiscal reforms stabilised revenue collection and protected peasants from arbitrary exactions by nomadic elites.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In Iran the Ilkhan <b>Ghazan</b> became a Muslim in 1295, and his minister Rashid al-Din compiled the Compendium of Chronicles, an attempt at a history of the whole known world that drew on Chinese, Persian, Arabic, Turkic, Indian, and European sources and informants.</blockquote><a href="/essays/cross-cultural-exchange">Cross-Cultural Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ilkhan <b>Ghazan</b> became a Muslim in 1295, and the Ilkhanate disintegrated after the death of Abu Sa'id without an heir in 1335; the Yuan were driven from China in 1368.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-collapse-problem">The Collapse Problem</a></li></ul></section>
